+/*
+ * S7: the thing that actually ate the touches.
+ *
+ * The S6 run went looking for a cover sheet and did not find one: the window stack of TEST22B/E
+ * (artifacts/vayu/test22-lead2-20261028, com.hakios.layers) has no SBCoverSheetWindow at all.
+ * What it does have, from boot on, is
+ *
+ *     window SBAlertItemWindow level 2000, context 0x5c18e2a6
+ *       _SBAlertController, two labels, two buttons, a full-screen 20% dimming view
+ *
+ * and backboardd's touchstats for every synthetic touch name exactly that context:
+ * "destinations: { SpringBoard.10 contextID 5C18E2A6: ... }". An alert item is modal: it makes
+ * SpringBoard's scene the active one and leaves the application's scene foreground-inactive, so
+ * the application is visible (its scene is foreground) but receives nothing and redraws nothing
+ * -- the "1 frame a second" of TEST20B is that, not the renderer.
+ *
+ * The item is SBActivationFailedAlertItem, and it comes from the fake modem: in SpringBoard 16.5
+ * (20F66) -[SBTelephonyManager _provisioningUpdateWithStatus:slot:] (0x1c4b29ef4) builds it with
+ * -initWithFailureCount:slot: (classref 0x1de7c9498 = SBActivationFailedAlertItem) and hands it
+ * to -[SBTelephonyManager _setCurrentActivationAlertItem:] (0x1c4b29e50), which deactivates the
+ * old item and calls -[SBAlertItemsController activateAlertItem:]. The fake SIM of vayu_cell
+ * never reports "activated", so the alert never goes away by itself and there is no human here
+ * to tap Try Again.
+ *
+ * So with boot-arg s7=1:
+ *   - _setCurrentActivationAlertItem: is replaced: an item is logged and dropped (the original is
+ *     called with nil, which is how it deactivates whatever was up);
+ *   - com.hakios.ready dismisses any alert that was already presented before the hook,
+ *     dismisses the application switcher (the prefs: launch leaves an unbalanced
+ *     SBSwitcherViewController behind), and, if it is up, presents the cover sheet as gone
+ *     through the real setter of SBCoverSheetPresentationManager;
+ *   - the same is done at +6 s and +12 s after SpringBoard starts and after every unlock.
+ * The methods are looked up by name and the state is printed before and after, so a run without
+ * s7=1 behaves as before and a run with it says exactly what it found.
+ */
